            "phase": "final_answer",
            "questions": null,
            "text": "{\"nodes\":[{\"node_id\":\"node-1\",\"text\":\"\\\\begin{proof}\",\"claim\":\"Begins the proof.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"scope\":[],\"goal_refs\":[\"main\"]},{\"node_id\":\"node-2\",\"text\":\"The sequence $\\\\left(f_n\\\\right)_n$ does not converge uniformly, since given $0<\\\\varepsilon<1$ and $N \\\\in \\\\mathbb{Z}_{+}$, for $x=\\\\varepsilon^{1 / N}$ we have $d\\\\left(f_N(x), f(x)\\\\right)=\\\\varepsilon$.\",\"claim\":\"Claims nonuniform convergence using the witness $n=N$ and $x=\\\\varepsilon^{1/N}$. The equality requires an unstated function $f$ with $f(x)=0$ for this $x<1$, as well as the usual metric; the proof has not defined or established that $f$ is the pointwise limit.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"scope\":[],\"goal_refs\":[\"main\"]},{\"node_id\":\"node-3\",\"text\":\"We can also apply Theorem 21.6: the convergence is not uniform since $f$ is not continuous.\",\"claim\":\"Offers a second argument based on the discontinuity of $f$. Its applicability cannot be checked because $f$ is undefined, its discontinuity is not established, and the statement of Theorem 21.6 is not supplied.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"scope\":[],\"goal_refs\":[\"main\"]},{\"node_id\":\"node-4\",\"text\":\"\\\\end{proof}\",\"claim\":\"Ends the proof.\",\"depends_on\":[\"node-2\",\"node-3\"],\"scope\":[],\"goal_refs\":[\"main\"]}],\"ambiguities\":[\"The symbol $f$ is never defined. The intended pointwise limit appears to be $f(x)=0$ for $0\\\\le x<1$ and $f(1)=1$, but this is neither stated nor proved.\",\"The metric $d$ is not specified; the displayed equality assumes the usual metric on $\\\\mathbb{R}$.\",\"The statement and hypotheses of Theorem 21.6 are unavailable, so its application cannot be verified from the supplied proof.\",\"Without identifying the pointwise limit, neither proposed argument completely establishes the main goal as written.\"]}",
            "type": "agentMessage"
          }
        ],
